WEBVTT

NOTE Narrated by a synthetic voice (an ElevenLabs stock voice); the opening disclosure is the macOS Trinoids voice. Text by Phil Renato.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.690
Cups &amp; Collections (or how to carry Diet Coke and keep your blood from boiling out

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:10.077
into a cold universe)

00:00:10.077 --> 00:00:15.836
“The past is hidden somewhere outside the realm, beyond the reach of the intellect,

00:00:15.836 --> 00:00:21.455
in some material object. In the sensation which the material object will give us,

00:00:21.455 --> 00:00:24.334
which we do not suspect.” — Marcel Proust.

00:00:24.334 --> 00:00:29.553
One of my very first memories is of a white plastic cup. I was three years old

00:00:29.553 --> 00:00:34.569
and woke up to the sounds of my parents playing cards with my mother’s best

00:00:34.569 --> 00:00:39.788
friend in our living room. I put on my tall rubber boots and proceeded to push

00:00:39.788 --> 00:00:44.939
my brother’s crib into the hallway — for a reason I can’t now remember. As it

00:00:44.939 --> 00:00:50.090
bumped the doorframe on the way out of our shared bedroom I heard him cry and

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:54.970
saw what seemed like gallons of red liquid pouring out of the side of the

00:00:54.970 --> 00:01:00.121
wood, wheeled, slatted crib. Fractions of a second later, out tumbled my cup;

00:01:00.121 --> 00:01:05.002
white, with a wide blue base and screen printed orange lions on the side,

00:01:05.002 --> 00:01:09.475
terminated by a spill-proof lid that wasn’t snapped on quite tight.

00:01:09.475 --> 00:01:15.330
In the 1970s, most Americans were not terribly discriminating in their interior

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:20.434
decorating impulse control — they chose wild, fuzzy colors, wallpaper

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:25.988
outornamented only by the Victorian and shag-carpet covered everything. Our

00:01:25.988 --> 00:01:31.993
carpet was technically tan — but it was considerably closer to 14karat yellow. My

00:01:31.993 --> 00:01:38.148
cup, now emptied of its Kool-Aid®, sat on its side, in perfect condition. An empty,

00:01:38.148 --> 00:01:43.853
glossy, stout tower; floating prone in the center of a now orange puddle that

00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:46.255
was growing wider by the second.

00:01:46.255 --> 00:01:50.618
In the years since, I’ve spilled or misplaced at least half the drinking

00:01:50.618 --> 00:01:55.227
vessels I’ve ever encountered. It’s usually not a klutzy in the moment loss,

00:01:55.227 --> 00:02:00.082
but the result of a slow loss of interest from the moment I pick up a new, cold,

00:02:00.082 --> 00:02:04.629
beautiful, pink can of Tab® to the flat, hot black liquid it becomes within

00:02:04.629 --> 00:02:09.177
minutes of opening it. “I’ll just leave it right here, and come back for it

00:02:09.177 --> 00:02:13.847
later.” But I don’t, and then someone bumps the table, and eventually my iced

00:02:13.847 --> 00:02:18.641
tea or Diet Mountain Dew or protein shake is something that has to be sucked up

00:02:18.641 --> 00:02:23.495
with a mop. And then I’m ready to grab something else, the cycle begins anew and

00:02:23.495 --> 00:02:28.043
I’m just lucky I don’t happen to like glass cups and bottles. What persists

00:02:28.043 --> 00:02:32.898
isn’t just the habit of not caring for what I’m consuming, but the attraction to

00:02:32.898 --> 00:02:35.110
the package, the ritual, the vessel.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:40.289
The first hoarding behavior I remember having was of a series of 1984 Coca-Cola®

00:02:40.289 --> 00:02:45.534
cans. The Detroit Tigers were to go on to win the World Series that year, and the

00:02:45.534 --> 00:02:50.648
strongest brand in the world decided to make a few extra dollars by putting the

00:02:50.648 --> 00:02:55.631
likenesses of the players on otherwise standard 12oz cans. Over a few weeks I

00:02:55.631 --> 00:03:00.876
was able to purchase one of each of the limited edition mementos, and I displayed

00:03:00.876 --> 00:03:06.055
them on the mantle of our fireplace. I had other toys and middle-class junk that

00:03:06.055 --> 00:03:11.038
other kids had, but I had collected these, personally. I had walked my chubby

00:03:11.038 --> 00:03:16.020
ass down to the Double Barrel Party Store, paid for them, and walked them the

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.069
half mile back to my suburban house. I had chosen the location to display them

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:26.117
and I was as proud of these as anything else I could be proud of at the age of

00:03:26.117 --> 00:03:26.379
ten.

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:32.023
But I hated, and hate, baseball. I wasn’t a fan of almost any sports — but of the

00:03:32.023 --> 00:03:37.526
ones I knew about in 1984, baseball was the one I liked least. It was slow, had

00:03:37.526 --> 00:03:41.970
eccentric rules, I was not good at it and my father liked to get

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:47.473
Doberman-punching-drunk while he watched it. Still, I was pissed when my little

00:03:47.473 --> 00:03:53.258
brother drank almost every can and placed them back on the mantle, in order, over a

00:03:53.258 --> 00:03:58.832
period of days without my noticing that they’d been opened. My property had been

00:03:58.832 --> 00:04:04.335
damaged, sure, but something I curated was ruined. It was as if I had designed,

00:04:04.335 --> 00:04:09.767
fabricated from sheet, and personally printed every single can. I got over it,

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:15.270
and flirted with collecting glasses at Arby’s with gold plated rims and antique

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:20.561
glass bottles of TowneClub® soda in wooden crates. But none of these nascent

00:04:20.561 --> 00:04:26.276
collections stuck. • What attracts us to something? Umberto Eco laid out dozens of

00:04:26.276 --> 00:04:31.778
scenarios in On Beauty; from imagined golden proportions, to Aquinas’ notion of

00:04:31.778 --> 00:04:36.717
natural orders, to modern tensions between the novel and the familiar —

00:04:36.717 --> 00:04:42.149
beauty is a big part of attraction. If I am viscerally affected by a form I am

00:04:42.149 --> 00:04:47.158
likely to engage it. If it is useful to me (or I imagine it to be,) that

00:04:47.158 --> 00:04:52.097
augmented behavior reinforces the relationship. And if the Visceral and

00:04:52.097 --> 00:04:57.035
Behavioral are strong enough, I may continue thinking about it and that

00:04:57.035 --> 00:05:02.679
relationship may (even subtly) change the way I interact with other things, other

00:05:02.679 --> 00:05:07.900
people — and this Reflective experience will transform an attraction into a

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:13.050
durable relationship. Sometimes even with abstract or unattainable things.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:18.431
Imagine three vehicles — a Ferrari, a Volvo and a Schwinn bicycle. Each of us

00:05:18.431 --> 00:05:23.882
brings significant prejudice to each of these — but we should be able to agree

00:05:23.882 --> 00:05:29.050
that one is largely about form, one about safety and one about efficiency.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:34.572
Particular forms, safety and efficiencies to be sure — a Ford truck may be more

00:05:34.572 --> 00:05:40.165
beautiful to some, traveling by air is safer than driving any car, and a bicycle

00:05:40.165 --> 00:05:45.829
is efficient so long as you don’t need to haul things. But part of the meaning of

00:05:45.829 --> 00:05:51.280
these things, some of why we would buy (or wish to own and use) these vehicles

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.732
is about (stimulating) form, (occupant) safety and (environmental) efficiency.

00:05:56.732 --> 00:06:01.971
This general dynamic holds even if we swap out these brands with particular

00:06:01.971 --> 00:06:07.705
models within the brands, and even if we switch to Porsche, Saab, and Shimano. But

00:06:07.705 --> 00:06:12.307
these brainds will have both parrallel and perpendicular meanings.

00:06:12.307 --> 00:06:17.799
The Ferrari is about speed, exclusivity, racing, luxury, pride, masculinity, a

00:06:17.799 --> 00:06:22.363
particular sound, conspicuous consumption, craftspersonship, drug

00:06:22.363 --> 00:06:27.498
dealers, Hawaii, rap videos, plastic models, Europe, snobs, weekends, the

00:06:27.498 --> 00:06:32.419
smell of burning rubber, Italy, privilege, wealth, sex, the color red,

00:06:32.419 --> 00:06:37.768
and dozens of other associations we could make, culturally and individually.

00:06:37.768 --> 00:06:43.545
Similar lists could be made for the Volvo and the Schwinn — and each person making

00:06:43.545 --> 00:06:48.751
these lists would emphasize different associations in order to advance the

00:06:48.751 --> 00:06:54.457
aspects that they think about most often when considering these vehicles. Where I

00:06:54.457 --> 00:06:59.663
might say ‘danger,’ someone else might write ‘exercise,’ and a third would

00:06:59.663 --> 00:07:05.083
advance ‘clean;’ when describing the Schwinn, for instance. And we may all be

00:07:05.083 --> 00:07:10.290
right, and may all still be pulled to this vehicle no matter our different

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:15.425
reasons — it’s magnetic, a force field where the material pulling and the

00:07:15.425 --> 00:07:20.274
material pulled must be made of compatible stuff. To say something is

00:07:20.274 --> 00:07:25.267
beautiful, or useful, is not unlike saying that a thing is natural — or

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:30.616
saying that something is organic when it’s made of plastic. These things are

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:33.112
true, but semantically situational.

00:07:33.112 --> 00:07:37.948
I can explain some of why a teapot attracts me. A particular pot has a

00:07:37.948 --> 00:07:43.555
compelling belly, spout, handle, material and surface. I like tea, and I like the

00:07:43.555 --> 00:07:49.021
ideas of tea — the leisure ritual, the accoutrements, the stimulation. But some

00:07:49.021 --> 00:07:54.488
of it defies my conscious understanding or control — why, in our age, do I need

00:07:54.488 --> 00:07:59.884
one? I can just put the bag in my cup, and it tastes the same to me (and would

00:07:59.884 --> 00:08:04.720
bet that the chemical effects of fine brewing are vastly overstated by

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:10.327
teasthetes.) The kinds of kettles I like are in a similar condition — I know that

00:08:10.327 --> 00:08:15.723
the most efficient way to heat water is in an electric kettle or a microwave —

00:08:15.723 --> 00:08:21.050
and yet I covet the stove top variety. It’s not just the particular, or class

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:26.376
of, kettles — it’s me. It’s a positive charge I’ve developed in a lifetime of

00:08:26.376 --> 00:08:31.632
watching (even infrequent) interactions with water being boiled in a kettle,

00:08:31.632 --> 00:08:37.169
transferred to a pot to steep tea, and then poured into a cup. It’s a cascade of

00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:42.495
pouring water, being transformed from hot to cold, clear to brown, from large

00:08:42.495 --> 00:08:47.681
reservoir to small, and from small to tiny — too small to sate my thirst in

00:08:47.681 --> 00:08:49.784
fewer than three to five cups.

00:08:49.784 --> 00:08:54.347
It is also a staple product of two different fields I identify with;

00:08:54.347 --> 00:08:59.726
traditional crafts and digital design. While the artist-madeone of a kind teapot

00:08:59.726 --> 00:09:04.766
is a common, and occasionally singular, expression of contemporary ceramic,

00:09:04.766 --> 00:09:10.009
metal, and glass craft artists, it is also a default character within computer

00:09:10.009 --> 00:09:14.912
graphics — one of the standard forms used in rendering and animation test

00:09:14.912 --> 00:09:20.292
batteries. In both cases it relies on a common cultural recognition and interest

00:09:20.292 --> 00:09:25.468
in the format, but where the Utah teapot is the most similar to its eccentric

00:09:25.468 --> 00:09:30.167
handmade cousin is in its frequency of use. There are potters who make

00:09:30.167 --> 00:09:35.410
reasonably priced functional teapots for use, but most are used exactly as the

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:40.177
mesh 3D model of the Utah teapot is — as an explicit symbol: of tea, of

00:09:40.177 --> 00:09:43.514
stimulation, and a host of other adjunct meanings.

00:09:43.514 --> 00:09:48.968
I don’t collect many teapots, but I collect many images of them in stores, in

00:09:48.968 --> 00:09:54.422
studios, and in the wild. I talk about them, I read about them. And I collect

00:09:54.422 --> 00:09:59.517
(images of and physical) vessels generally. I don’t own a Ferrari, but I

00:09:59.517 --> 00:10:04.900
run up on the few I see and molest them with my camera. I read about them, I

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:10.497
write about them, I talk about them. And I own, collect, and seek out and study

00:10:10.497 --> 00:10:16.310
cars in general that owe debts to Ferrari and the meanings of Ferrari. I draw from

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:21.405
these things — although usually not directly from their images. Volumes,

00:10:21.405 --> 00:10:26.716
grounding, entrances, contents, surfaces, exits, handles, speed, materials,

00:10:26.716 --> 00:10:32.098
familiarity, detail, function, process, color, surprise, creases, lifestyle;

00:10:32.098 --> 00:10:35.758
these show up in almost everything I think and make.

00:10:35.758 --> 00:10:39.102
But images of the Volvo or the Schwinn never show up.

00:10:39.102 --> 00:10:43.766
Our collection behaviors manifest for very different reasons. Some are

00:10:43.766 --> 00:10:48.836
persistently connected to dreams from our childhoods, some are thought of as

00:10:48.836 --> 00:10:54.176
investments, many dip into compulsion, others come from the shock of having lost

00:10:54.176 --> 00:10:58.908
something or someone. Last year I listened to an audiobook called Salt,

00:10:58.908 --> 00:11:04.383
Sugar, Fat. As the narrator read Michael Moss’s book to me, about the interplay of

00:11:04.383 --> 00:11:09.318
these compounds in the chemistry, marketing and addictions of modern food,

00:11:09.318 --> 00:11:14.590
I found myself thinking about ingredients more than the morals of this story. I

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:19.863
would walk into my studio, earbuds on, and imagine that my shelves of books and

00:11:19.863 --> 00:11:25.203
objects were base materials. Some of them could be refined into new compounds to

00:11:25.203 --> 00:11:30.205
achieve different ends; to make a thing more desirable, to give it mass and

00:11:30.205 --> 00:11:35.478
depth, or to keep the thing I was making from spoiling. Sugar, fat, salt. I was

00:11:35.478 --> 00:11:40.548
grasping for a triparte structure, because that’s what the book was pounding

00:11:40.548 --> 00:11:44.536
into me, but it seemed fairly isomorphic to my own practice.

00:11:44.536 --> 00:11:49.437
The inputs can be between raw and completely broken down to elements, each

00:11:49.437 --> 00:11:54.339
can be expressed with near dominant purity or be mixed with other elements

00:11:54.339 --> 00:11:59.442
until a ‘bliss point’ is reached in our pleasure centers and extremities. And

00:11:59.442 --> 00:12:04.478
there is an interplay of the chemistry and the language and other peripheral

00:12:04.478 --> 00:12:09.446
agents we employ to engage people with our work. I can follow either of the

00:12:09.446 --> 00:12:14.885
polar recommendations of the book — keep it pure and healthy and in moderation, or

00:12:14.885 --> 00:12:19.383
use the agricultural and giant food company model of calculating and

00:12:19.383 --> 00:12:24.151
preparing products designed for high consumption and profit, rather than

00:12:24.151 --> 00:12:29.119
nutrition. Or something in between the two. If the former is a left lane of

00:12:29.119 --> 00:12:33.752
traditional techniques and basic materials with time tested historical

00:12:33.752 --> 00:12:39.124
formal ideas, and the latter is the right lane — filled with the newest tools and

00:12:39.124 --> 00:12:44.227
materials and formats, capturing the popular imagination, I think I’ve chosen

00:12:44.227 --> 00:12:49.397
a windy path between and around. It’s a straw man, but one that I like beating

00:12:49.397 --> 00:12:49.598
on.

00:12:49.598 --> 00:12:55.782
“The unpredictable and the predetermined unfold together to make everything the

00:12:55.782 --> 00:13:01.226
way it is. It’s how nature creates itself on every scale, the snowflake and the

00:13:01.226 --> 00:13:01.994
snowstorm”.

00:13:01.994 --> 00:13:09.099
Footnote 7. Arcadia, Tom Stoppard, 1993.

00:13:09.099 --> 00:13:14.282
We swim in chemical culture, through oceans of dominant groups and seas of

00:13:14.282 --> 00:13:19.535
subcults. We extract oxygen by filtering out the miasmatic excrement of our

00:13:19.535 --> 00:13:24.718
children, friends and strangers. Our liquor dilutes it and numbs us to the

00:13:24.718 --> 00:13:30.184
taste, our drugs cut the infection with manic calm and our french fries absorb

00:13:30.184 --> 00:13:35.935
and help us pass the waste on. Our graves are earth, precipitated from broken down

00:13:35.935 --> 00:13:41.472
bottle caps; our universities built on the taxes and philanthropy of those kept

00:13:41.472 --> 00:13:46.655
alive on drips of tobacco-potato colloids. Chemistry is matter, culture is

00:13:46.655 --> 00:13:52.050
representation. Our suspension includes both elements as symbols and signs as

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:56.736
atoms; pools of biohazards and astringents spat from saxophones and

00:13:56.736 --> 00:13:57.304
siphons.

00:13:57.304 --> 00:14:02.563
How will we recognize our Handmaid’s Tale? What will our great grandchildren

00:14:02.563 --> 00:14:07.822
cast and emboss for tourists, wear as their wealth, or burnish in buckets to

00:14:07.822 --> 00:14:12.940
commemorate our origin myths? We have plenty of plastic, plywood and pig’s

00:14:12.940 --> 00:14:18.059
blood — but who doesn’t? What is nearsclusively American, brandable and so

00:14:18.059 --> 00:14:23.037
two-thousand-and-teens that it will translate into eternity? Combining a

00:14:23.037 --> 00:14:28.296
McNugget, an iPhone and an enameled silver Facebook thumb is too shallow and

00:14:28.296 --> 00:14:33.905
too deep a pastiche. A pitted, chrome lug nut, rolled in bacon is too dull. Maybe

00:14:33.905 --> 00:14:38.673
tiny, golden dildos, made from spent brass bullet shells, etched with

00:14:38.673 --> 00:14:43.581
Confederate flags, filled with mercury-bone amalgam. When we are issued

00:14:43.581 --> 00:14:48.910
these tokens, in paper bags, velvet boxes, or blister packs, we’ll know we’ve

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:50.663
made it safely to Gilead.

00:14:50.663 --> 00:14:56.452
In the beginning there was Chronos and Ananke, time and force, coalesced into

00:14:56.452 --> 00:15:01.936
the form of a serpent. They encircled a cosmic egg made of molten silver;

00:15:01.936 --> 00:15:07.572
reflecting nothing. Squeezing it, they slowed and split the world; cracking

00:15:07.572 --> 00:15:13.437
sound that would eventually condense as Echo. Chaos, Erebus and Aether floated

00:15:13.437 --> 00:15:19.150
out to rule space, darkness and light, while Gaia and Oceanus fell to become

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:24.786
earth and sea. Olympus rose above the night and became a font of gods; each

00:15:24.786 --> 00:15:27.909
bored of the new day’s unblemished beauty.

00:15:27.909 --> 00:15:32.982
While these Titans quarreled, castrated and killed one another over their

00:15:32.982 --> 00:15:38.689
infinite domains, young Prometheus shaped mud into man. He stood his new creatures

00:15:38.689 --> 00:15:44.325
upright and gave them torches dipped in Helios’ sun. But this technology couldn’t

00:15:44.325 --> 00:15:49.891
save them — Chronos would take the life of each in time for his wife to crush as

00:15:49.891 --> 00:15:50.525
diamonds.

00:15:50.525 --> 00:15:56.147
The first woman, Pandora, held a small jar given to her as a gift by Zeus. It

00:15:56.147 --> 00:16:01.843
was clay, baked in fire, smashed and put back together, pulverized and remade,

00:16:01.843 --> 00:16:07.686
ground and reassembled with a paste of lacquer and gold dust. She lifted the lid

00:16:07.686 --> 00:16:13.382
carefully and found what seemed only shadow. Beneath — small pieces of silver;

00:16:13.382 --> 00:16:18.856
bright glisters of hope that her race might one day expect to live forever.

00:16:18.856 --> 00:16:23.590
And, with that, despair that this torment might be everlasting...

00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:28.871
Hope is a shovel packing feces into buckets, carried from one bed to the

00:16:28.871 --> 00:16:34.747
next; troweling it out onto open sores and burnishing bandages. It tells me that

00:16:34.747 --> 00:16:40.177
the next twelve thousand days could possibly repair the last and that some

00:16:40.177 --> 00:16:45.756
object, lover, or child could bring me back to play. It quiets the fear that

00:16:45.756 --> 00:16:51.111
busy hands are somewhere choking fairness. It buries the doubt that other

00:16:51.111 --> 00:16:56.839
eyes look on yours to see beyond their own reflection. It wrings every drop of

00:16:56.839 --> 00:17:02.789
fluid from your body and sprays it out in celebration at the least success. It is

00:17:02.789 --> 00:17:07.996
the only palatable excuse for the “creation of works derived solely for

00:17:07.996 --> 00:17:13.798
pleasure.” It performs for an audience occasionally distracted (or consumed) by

00:17:13.798 --> 00:17:19.153
forms at once compulsive, useful, and transforming. I am resolution; near

00:17:19.153 --> 00:17:24.955
bursting from the weight of so many false starts, just short of shutting up and

00:17:24.955 --> 00:17:26.517
getting back to work.

00:17:26.517 --> 00:17:31.730
Chrome on detailed or compound surfaces scrambles the corpus callosum; wringing

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:36.341
this tough body of neural fibers until its messages slip, not from one

00:17:36.341 --> 00:17:41.820
hemisphere to another, but back and forth within the membrane until their energy is

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:46.899
expended; a loop for rays. These bright bounces are semifluids, mirroring the

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:51.778
world in rubber and fractions — making the forms they shroud XY tables for

00:17:51.778 --> 00:17:56.923
critical angles of incidence. Cutting in, we might move back through chromium,

00:17:56.923 --> 00:18:02.270
nickel, copper and steel, or through the flakes of clear polyurethane, flashes of

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:07.348
aluminum vapor and black styrene, or maybe even grind through polished silver

00:18:07.348 --> 00:18:12.561
or stainless directly. The mechanics are always the same — they aren’t mirrors,

00:18:12.561 --> 00:18:17.773
but optopathic tiles. Where the mirror reflects and distorts the world in ways

00:18:17.773 --> 00:18:22.451
that junior geometers can calculate, the mirrormosaic splits the self —

00:18:22.451 --> 00:18:27.129
transferring the functions of representation to a suborgan incapable of

00:18:27.129 --> 00:18:29.535
passing the pieces on to the solver.

00:18:29.535 --> 00:18:35.185
The is a transporter, etching out what little substance we have and projecting

00:18:35.185 --> 00:18:40.175
us through space over time. The woman with the golden lingerie is not

00:18:40.175 --> 00:18:45.312
continuous; she’s made of space — occasionally populated by tiny masses

00:18:45.312 --> 00:18:51.035
with orbiting, interacting force fields. While gold can be hammered until it is

00:18:51.035 --> 00:18:56.612
translucent, her brassiere is made of fibers spun with metallic scintillants.

00:18:56.612 --> 00:19:02.409
The warps are made from mercerized wood pulp with a high wet modulus that’s been

00:19:02.409 --> 00:19:07.032
loaded with ochre pigments and mica flake. The weft is made from

00:19:07.032 --> 00:19:12.462
semi-aromatic polyamide condensation copolymer chains that have been dipped

00:19:12.462 --> 00:19:18.406
aniline yellow. The photographer shot her in emulsive grain, screens dissolved her

00:19:18.406 --> 00:19:24.203
into AM or PM dots, the plate picked up ink wherever it wasn’t wet and the press

00:19:24.203 --> 00:19:29.927
put her back together as it kissed paper in C, M, Y and K. Subjects and objects

00:19:29.927 --> 00:19:35.430
are all broken, fields of divisible particles that we have learned to see as

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:41.007
individuals, immutable and noble. The vibrations of reassembly are seduction.

00:19:41.007 --> 00:19:46.982
“Rose gold is decadent. It is gold for people who already have enough gold

00:19:46.982 --> 00:19:47.555
gold.”.

00:19:47.555 --> 00:19:51.177
“Sparkle like a gem with a golden voice.”.

00:19:51.177 --> 00:19:56.332
“I want to take off my silver earrings… rid of all the garbage.”.

00:19:56.332 --> 00:20:00.047
“Lindsay hadn’t heard the world jewelry in a long time.”.

00:20:00.047 --> 00:20:05.502
“I’ve never seen a diamond in the flesh, I cut my teeth on wedding rings in the

00:20:05.502 --> 00:20:06.131
movies.”.

00:20:06.131 --> 00:20:08.778
“Jewelry making is not a manly subject,

00:20:08.778 --> 00:20:11.100
where guns are.”.

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:16.356
“Poets and travelers dreamed of splendid castles made of marble and gemstones.

00:20:16.356 --> 00:20:21.612
They invented them... described them as they ought to have been in order to be

00:20:21.612 --> 00:20:22.431
beautiful.”.

00:20:22.431 --> 00:20:28.103
“All your silver rings, and all your silken things...that song you sing is

00:20:28.103 --> 00:20:30.279
keeping you from breaking.”.

00:20:30.279 --> 00:20:33.716
“I’m just trying to change the color on your mood ring.”.

00:20:33.716 --> 00:20:39.305
“My hopes are so high that your kiss might kill me... My heart is yours to

00:20:39.305 --> 00:20:45.047
fill or burst, to break or bury, or wear as jewelry. Whichever you prefer.”.

00:20:45.047 --> 00:20:49.784
“And the rings “fall out like a silver snowfall into the blinding light.”.

00:20:49.784 --> 00:20:53.174
“I want a ring so big that it gives me back problems.”.

00:20:53.174 --> 00:20:56.657
“She’s a dancing zookeeper’s daughter who makes jewelry.”.

00:20:56.657 --> 00:21:01.839
“Disfigured by low booths, built adjoining to the walls of the church,

00:21:01.839 --> 00:21:03.716
possessed by jewellers.”.

00:21:03.716 --> 00:21:07.803
“He was found with the jewelry and headdress he once wore.”.

00:21:07.803 --> 00:21:10.868
“I believe that jewelry needs balls.”.

00:21:10.868 --> 00:21:16.589
“You could have it all: the cars, the clothes, the jewels, the sex, the house.

00:21:16.589 --> 00:21:18.670
Baby, you could be a star.”.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:23.828
“Hildegard talks of a Lucifer (before the fall) adorned with gems that sparkled

00:21:23.828 --> 00:21:29.118
like a starry sky, so that the countless glittering beams that shone out from the

00:21:29.118 --> 00:21:34.078
splendour of all his ornaments flooded the world with light (Liber divinorum

00:21:34.078 --> 00:21:34.739
operum.)”.

00:21:34.739 --> 00:21:38.501
“Only the jeweler knows the nature of the lost jewel.”.

00:21:38.501 --> 00:21:43.050
“The fighters initially agreed and laid out a blanket, where Ms. Saleh placed her

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:47.371
heart-shaped pendant and her gold rings, while the men left crumpled bills.”.

00:21:47.371 --> 00:21:53.210
“Heart shaped jewelry is not something women buy for themselves.” “But you like

00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:58.749
heart-shaped jewelry right?” “No. Except for the pendant that you got me.”.

00:21:58.749 --> 00:22:03.427
“Why would anyone want to put a metal ring through the end of their prick?”

00:22:03.427 --> 00:22:05.576
“What are you looking at me for?”.

00:22:05.576 --> 00:22:09.570
“100 jewels between the teeth.”.

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:12.728
“This is my grandmother’s buttock bullet ring.”.

00:22:12.728 --> 00:22:15.422
“You could always model ladies jewelry.”.

00:22:15.422 --> 00:22:18.533
“I can’t keep spinning gold out of your ass.”.

00:22:18.533 --> 00:22:21.830
“You like diamond rings, I can provide.”.

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:28.433
“To distill so specific a form, from all that chaos. It’s like turning air into

00:22:28.433 --> 00:22:29.957
gold. A miracle.”.

00:22:29.957 --> 00:22:34.229
“She used to have to work at her dad’s jewelry factory if she was bad.”.

00:22:34.229 --> 00:22:39.570
“I’ve got the magic in me, everytime I touch that track it turns into gold.”.

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:42.449
“Sparkle like a gem with a golden voice.”.

00:22:42.449 --> 00:22:46.536
“All the gold and the guns and the girls couldn’t get you off...”.

00:22:46.536 --> 00:22:50.205
“Like, the opposite of gold.”.

00:22:50.205 --> 00:22:54.106
“My feet are tired of wearing your golden crown around town!”.

00:22:54.106 --> 00:22:58.378
“And the scars that mark my body, they are silver and gold.”.

00:22:58.378 --> 00:23:04.090
“It’s just an illusion, here on earth, that one moment follows another one —

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:05.994
like beads on a string.”.

00:23:05.994 --> 00:23:10.127
“The ring yearns to return to the hand of its master.”.

00:23:10.127 --> 00:23:15.096
“I hope to inspire children to be obsessed over jewelry.”.

00:23:15.096 --> 00:23:17.790
“Burning a hole in my pocket is this ring.”.

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:22.945
“Girl, we go together like rubber on chrome… Diamonds on gold.”.

00:23:22.945 --> 00:23:28.517
“Charlotte’s amethyst bracelet; fashioned out of the intertwining hair of her two

00:23:28.517 --> 00:23:29.214
sisters.”.

00:23:29.214 --> 00:23:34.676
“My hopes are so high that your kiss might kill me... My heart is yours to

00:23:34.676 --> 00:23:39.988
fill or burst To break or bury Or wear as jewelry Whichever you prefer”.

00:23:39.988 --> 00:23:43.053
“You’re going to want to look through the jewelry and nails tray.

00:23:43.053 --> 00:23:48.161
There is some beautiful jewelry, but be careful, there’s also nails.”.

00:23:48.161 --> 00:23:52.526
“The photograph was just his hands with a bracelet of flowers around it.”.

00:23:52.526 --> 00:23:58.842
“Did that bird just say my name?” “Yeah it did, right before it crapped gold.”.

00:23:58.842 --> 00:24:04.452
“Uh, she’s going to make me buy some of her homemade jewelry. Birds always attack

00:24:04.452 --> 00:24:05.854
me when I wear it.”.

00:24:05.854 --> 00:24:11.113
“Healer, gun enthusiast, jewelry designer; there is nothing this man can’t

00:24:11.113 --> 00:24:11.473
do.”.

00:24:11.473 --> 00:24:16.810
“I had a dream I could buy my way to heaven. When I awoke I spent that on a

00:24:16.810 --> 00:24:17.603
necklace…”.

00:24:17.603 --> 00:24:20.668
“Is that falcon wearing a necklace?”.

00:24:20.668 --> 00:24:26.290
“How is it that the prize for winning the most macho game in the most macho sport

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:28.749
in history is a piece of jewelry?”.

00:24:28.749 --> 00:24:33.478
“You can fit me inside the necklace you got when you were sixteen. Next to your

00:24:33.478 --> 00:24:35.297
heartbeat where I should be.”.

00:24:35.297 --> 00:24:39.709
“You don’t win silver, you lose gold.”.

00:24:39.709 --> 00:24:43.099
“Declaring the contents to be a necklace”.

00:24:43.099 --> 00:24:46.907
“They gave the two young women silver dog tags.”.

00:24:46.907 --> 00:24:49.833
“I’ve seen this diamond cut through harder men”.

00:24:49.833 --> 00:24:54.648
“It is a fact that the diamonds were there, for they have been discovered and

00:24:54.648 --> 00:24:59.526
sold; and that they were carried down there during the drift period, from some

00:24:59.526 --> 00:25:00.793
northern locality.”.

00:25:00.793 --> 00:25:04.973
“She’s showing me how to make jewelry, which earns her extra cash.”.

00:25:04.973 --> 00:25:06.877
“Can I listen to your necklace?”.

00:25:06.877 --> 00:25:10.732
“The old silver jewelry smell of oncoming rain.”.

00:25:10.732 --> 00:25:16.180
“Yellow was considered the color of cowardice and was associated with pariahs

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:21.484
and social outcasts, the insane, Muslims and Jews; nevertheless it was also

00:25:21.484 --> 00:25:26.789
celebrated as the color of gold, seen as the brightest and most precious of

00:25:26.789 --> 00:25:32.595
metals.” “Doctors and naive love, silver spoons over fire. You tell a lazy lie and

00:25:32.595 --> 00:25:34.602
I tell them you’re a liar.”.

00:25:34.602 --> 00:25:38.131
“That’s why they sell a wine glass holder necklace.”.

00:25:38.131 --> 00:25:42.024
“You’re lucky we aren’t back in oldentimes, I would make a necklace out

00:25:42.024 --> 00:25:42.914
of your teeth.”.

00:25:42.914 --> 00:25:46.258
“Plant my lips where your necklaces close.”.

00:25:46.258 --> 00:25:52.083
“The species will generally be preserved in children. But as all are of the same

00:25:52.083 --> 00:25:57.686
original stock, a golden parent will sometimes have a silver son, or a silver

00:25:57.686 --> 00:25:59.308
parent a golden son.”.

00:25:59.308 --> 00:26:04.526
“We should feed our jewelry to the sea. For diamonds do appear to be just like

00:26:04.526 --> 00:26:05.949
broken glass to me.”.

00:26:05.949 --> 00:26:10.219
“Do you guys know where I could get one of those gold necklaces with a ‘t’ on

00:26:10.219 --> 00:26:10.500
it?”.

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:13.937
“You can tell I got cake from the diamonds in my teeth.”.

00:26:13.937 --> 00:26:18.163
“A necklace of follicles with sabertooth monocles”.

00:26:18.163 --> 00:26:23.406
“It’s a racket; I think they print those charts blurry and then tell you you need

00:26:23.406 --> 00:26:24.061
glasses.”.

00:26:24.061 --> 00:26:27.823
“I’m not going to apologize. It wasn’t even a real tiara.”.

00:26:27.823 --> 00:26:32.896
“That is a really great example of a horrible idea...my time is like a toddler

00:26:32.896 --> 00:26:35.532
in a tiara, I need really great ideas.”.

00:26:35.532 --> 00:26:40.557
“Wear me like a locket around your throat, I’ll weigh you down, I’ll watch

00:26:40.557 --> 00:26:41.383
you choke.”.

00:26:41.383 --> 00:26:46.120
“The King went and misplaced his crown, but it’ll come around.”.

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:49.278
“He is the brooch and gem of all the nation.”.

00:26:49.278 --> 00:26:53.597
“I’m the king of this pity party with my jewel encrusted crown.”.

00:26:53.597 --> 00:26:58.924
“The sons of major kings, when being fostered, should have “brooches of gold

00:26:58.924 --> 00:27:04.393
having crystal inserted in them,” while the sons of minor kings need wear only

00:27:04.393 --> 00:27:05.671
silver brooches.”.

00:27:05.671 --> 00:27:09.804
“The wings don’t make you fly and the crown don’t make you king...”.

00:27:09.804 --> 00:27:15.264
“Sometimes he wore a tiepin, or a brooch, or something consisting of two horizontal

00:27:15.264 --> 00:27:18.860
bars with a platinum figure bent complexly over both.”.

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:22.575
“From her clothes he ripped the golden brooches...”.

00:27:22.575 --> 00:27:27.451
“...Through which the bones shone like the points on a crown of thorns, or the

00:27:27.451 --> 00:27:28.844
beads of a rosary...”.

00:27:28.844 --> 00:27:31.352
“A crown of wet cigars.”.

00:27:31.352 --> 00:27:36.580
“That crown I got from being prom king is so tacky — I hardly even wear it

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:37.296
anymore.”.

00:27:37.296 --> 00:27:40.965
“Brooms be on the street now sweeping up the broken crowns.”.

00:27:40.965 --> 00:27:44.402
“If you take away her crown she’s just an old lady.”.

00:27:44.402 --> 00:27:49.007
“One delights in getting honors and crowns through storm-footed horses,

00:27:49.007 --> 00:27:53.942
Another in passing life in rooms rich in gold, Another still, safe traveling

00:27:53.942 --> 00:27:57.034
enjoys, in a swift ship, on a wave of the sea.”.

00:27:57.034 --> 00:28:00.053
“I know you love it when the hairpins start to drop.”.

00:28:00.053 --> 00:28:03.025
“You can wear the crown but you’re no princess.”.

00:28:03.025 --> 00:28:04.790
“Their hands and faces were,

00:28:04.790 --> 00:28:06.880
all badged with blood.”.

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:12.440
“And they alone of all the citizens may not touch or handle silver or gold, or be

00:28:12.440 --> 00:28:16.818
under the same roof as them, or wear them, or drink from them.”.

00:28:16.818 --> 00:28:19.976
“What’s the difference between gold and golden?”.

00:28:19.976 --> 00:28:22.112
“Carry this picture for luck,

00:28:22.112 --> 00:28:23.366
Kept in a locket,

00:28:23.366 --> 00:28:24.852
Tucked in your collar,

00:28:24.852 --> 00:28:26.524
Close to your chest.”.

00:28:26.524 --> 00:28:31.818
“They’re building an atomic bomb.” “You got that from a badge?”.

00:28:31.818 --> 00:28:36.611
Aesthetic is the differentiator — between ours and theirs, domestic and

00:28:36.611 --> 00:28:41.130
professional, his and hers… Even relatively new things have nuanced

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:46.059
variants for granular market segments, each filled with people who are so

00:28:46.059 --> 00:28:51.400
confused as to believe that they have individual preferences. Shelves have been

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:56.055
growing longer, getting taller, supporting increasing numbers of thin

00:28:56.055 --> 00:29:01.396
products, some of which are standing with their chests out so far that they are

00:29:01.396 --> 00:29:06.531
falling into aisles. But things like grooming products, weapons and sex toys

00:29:06.531 --> 00:29:11.803
have been collapsing into a unified form language. While there are still color

00:29:11.803 --> 00:29:17.280
choices, varying measures of capacity and segmented brands to appeal to different

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:22.278
shoppers — they can easily be mapped to one another across departments and

00:29:22.278 --> 00:29:27.345
stores, compared as commodities as swappable as sand. Even that tiny, macho

00:29:27.345 --> 00:29:32.617
gun shop down the street from your house will sell you a pistol in 5 different

00:29:32.617 --> 00:29:37.683
finishes, with a grip that makes your finger feel like it is under panties.

00:29:37.683 --> 00:29:42.476
They will come in beautiful boxes that boast about your newly purchased

00:29:42.476 --> 00:29:47.885
strength, like pheromones in body spray. This emerging mass will eventually pull

00:29:47.885 --> 00:29:53.088
in greeting cards and coffee; while things will continue working as they have

00:29:53.088 --> 00:29:57.128
been, to the end, all climaxing in the same ecstatic blasts.

00:29:57.128 --> 00:30:02.299
We are disappointments to our spirit animals. We conjure them when we need

00:30:02.299 --> 00:30:07.611
their strengths, but we are never as wise, courageous, or as cunning as they

00:30:07.611 --> 00:30:12.923
are — unless we’ve had brain injuries. Any reasonably constructed animism is

00:30:12.923 --> 00:30:18.164
omnipotent; and as soon as we start filling ourselves with these creatures,

00:30:18.164 --> 00:30:23.831
their pulp pours out of our cells. They aren’t just too effuse, too perfect, they

00:30:23.831 --> 00:30:29.214
are structurally beyond the problems we set them to solve. They can fix us by

00:30:29.214 --> 00:30:34.526
fiat before the symptoms even show up. But we can’t restore ourselves — even

00:30:34.526 --> 00:30:40.192
through them — we are humans, unable to use our towering intellects to repair the

00:30:40.192 --> 00:30:45.434
rippling cracks. In a universe of spirits: as our speed increases, we store

00:30:45.434 --> 00:30:50.392
our psychic energy as mass, requiring more energetic spirits to produce

00:30:50.392 --> 00:30:55.775
acceleration, which stalls us, requiring faster aids, who add to our failures

00:30:55.775 --> 00:30:56.200
and...
